  "result": "I opened the issue: https://github.com/acme/textstats/issues/13. I didn't change any code.\n\nI ran `word_count` first to confirm the bug. It returns `1` for `\"\"` and `4` for `\"   \"` (three spaces).\n\nThe cause is in `textstats/__init__.py:6`, which uses `len(text.split(\" \"))`. Splitting on an explicit `\" \"` always returns at least one item, so an empty string gives `[\"\"]` and a count of 1. Each extra space also adds an empty item, which is why whitespace-only text is miscounted.\n\nThe issue includes:\n- the steps to reproduce\n- the cause\n- the expected result: 0 for empty or whitespace-only text\n- a suggested fix: `len(text.split())`, which `average_word_length` in the same file already uses\n- a suggestion to add a regression test, since the only existing test checks `\"the quick brown fox\"`",
